From 219a07dd12c05f0d3aa7ce82739a9dcfb60a779c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Galih Tama <galpt@v.recipes>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 18:25:16 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 40/49] v4.8-gpu: drop the pick_next_entity WARN

pick_eevdf() legitimately returns NULL when every queued entity is
ineligible -- the protect path and the tight-deadline windows produced by
the futex/IPC vslice reductions.  The WARN_ON_ONCE(nr_queued > 0) was
therefore a spurious alarm on a correct path.  Drop it and document the
fallback to the leftmost queued entity, which keeps runnable tasks from
being skipped rather than idling.

Signed-off-by: Galih Tama <galpt@v.recipes>
---
 kernel/sched/fair.c | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/kernel/sched/fair.c b/kernel/sched/fair.c
index 835561208..043e4144c 100644
--- a/kernel/sched/fair.c
+++ b/kernel/sched/fair.c
@@ -5748,7 +5748,12 @@ pick_next_entity(struct rq *rq, struct cfs_rq *cfs_rq)
 
 	se = pick_eevdf(cfs_rq);
 	if (unlikely(!se)) {
-		WARN_ON_ONCE(cfs_rq->nr_queued > 0);
+		/* pick_eevdf() legitimately returns NULL when every queued
+		 * entity is ineligible (protect path, tight-deadline windows
+		 * from the futex/IPC vslice reductions).  Falling back to the
+		 * leftmost queued entity keeps runnable tasks from being
+		 * skipped rather than idling.
+		 */
 		se = __pick_first_entity(cfs_rq);
 		if (!se)
 			return NULL;
-- 
